chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 15 of the 3rd edition teaches how a Python program reads and writes Google Sheets spreadsheets with the third-party module EZSheets: it sets up access in the Google Cloud console (a project, the Sheets and Drive APIs, an OAuth consent screen, a credentials file, and two token files written at the first sign-in), opens, creates, uploads, downloads, lists and deletes spreadsheets, reads and writes the cells, rows and columns of their sheets, creates, moves, deletes, clears and copies sheets, builds one project (Project 11, a fake blockchain called Boringcoin that is audited in Step 1 and extended in Step 2), ends on quotas, and closes with 10 practice questions and three practice programs (Google Forms responses, a format converter and a check of a spreadsheet of bean counts) [P01] [P04] [P06]. The chapter was fetched on 2026-10-12 (85,632 bytes; 34 listings, 6 figures, 1 table) and its SHA-256, 76d2dc655b248a48921855f81131b8e4eee296340ed77ae0423cd48b11166d1b, equals the chapterDigest recorded in the book's own chapter ontology; the commit the course pins was not opened. EZSheets itself is a one-author library first uploaded to the Python Package Index on 2019-02-08, with 18 releases, the newest 2026.4.28 [P03]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P04, chx:P06 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "What can be run without a Google account reproduces. Under Python 3.14.6 with ezsheets 2026.4.28, google-api-python-client 2.201.0 and a stand-in for Google's HTTP answers (written for this study, because no Google account, OAuth consent or live API call was possible), the library's own requests and its handling of answers matched the printed shell sessions of the chapter for getColumnLetterOf(999) = 'ALK', getColumnNumberOf('ZZZ') = 18278, the empty string for an empty cell, the 1-based rows, the tab order after index changes, the deletions, the equality of the three ways of opening a spreadsheet and the 'Cannot delete all sheets' refusal. Project 11, Step 1, run whole on the real Boringcoin sheet (its CSV export of 2026-10-12, 1,001 rows, byte-identical to the export committed on 2026-10-01) printed the chapter's twenty balances, beginning 'Al Sweigart': 999058553, and Total Boringcoins: 1000000000; the shipped auditBoringcoin.py ran unchanged and printed the same [P01] [P58] [P03]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P58 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Several printed results of the chapter are not what the current library gives. Spreadsheet.Sheet(), which the chapter's listings use to make sheets and print Sheet objects, was added in release 2024.8.8 as a wrapper that returns nothing, so ss.Sheet('Spam') gives None, and only createSheet() returns the Sheet [P03]. After sheet['B2'] = 30 the chapter prints '30'; release 2024.8.9 converted written values to text in the local copy, release 2025.11.11 removed that, and release 2026.4.28 returns the integer 30 until the sheet is refreshed (the same session printed '30' under 2024.8.9 and 30 under the other two). The default download name replaces every character other than letters, digits, underscore and dot, so 'Sweigart Books (DO NOT DELETE)' is saved as Sweigart_Books_(DO_NOT_DELETE).xlsx and not as Sweigart_Books.xlsx. The real produceSales3.xlsx has 4 columns, where the chapter prints rows of 6 after the upload to Google. The page also has a stray space in 'token-sheets .pickle', says 'two token JSON files' for two pickles, and the alt text of Figure 15-2 names the cell A1 as AI [P01] [P03]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The short links of the chapter (autbor.com/boringcoin, autbor.com/examplegs) depend on a plain-HTTP request that the library sends itself. For any address that does not start with https://docs.google.com, Spreadsheet() follows redirects with http.client.HTTPConnection, which uses port 80 and sends no User-Agent. On 2026-10-12 autbor.com answered such requests 302 or 403 by turns: of ten requests made the library's way, 2 were answered 403 in the recorded run (5 and 4 in two earlier runs of the same check on the day), 5 of ten that carried only an Accept header, and none of ten that carried a User-Agent. A 403 leaves the address None and the library stops with AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'lower', before any Google call; in the recorded run the shipped addBoringcoinTransaction.py, with an Accept header added in memory, exited 1 in 2 of 5 runs for that reason, and the shipped auditBoringcoin.py exited 1 in earlier runs. The sheets themselves answer with a redirect to docs.google.com over both http and https [P01] [P42] [P58] [P60]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P42, chx:P58, chx:P60 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's account of quotas does not match Google's page or the library. It says a user may create 250 new spreadsheets a day and that free accounts can make a few hundred requests a minute; Google's Sheets usage-limits page (updated 2026-09-03) gives 300 read and 300 write requests a minute per project and 60 of each per user per project, has no daily limit beside the per-minute quotas and no figure for new spreadsheets, answers excess with 429 and advises truncated exponential backoff, recommends a payload of at most 2 MB, and says that exceeding the quotas is planned to incur charges later in 2026; the Drive limits changed on May 1, 2026 [P07] [P25]. The library retries only an error whose status is RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED: it pauses 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40 and 45 seconds (220 in all, not 'a minute or two'), tries nine times and raises HttpError, and raises any other error at once. ezsheets.IGNORE_QUOTA switches off only the library's own throttle of 90 writes or reads in 101 seconds, so the retry is identical with the flag set, and its comment (50 requests per 100 seconds) is out of date [P01] [P07] [P25]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07, chx:P25 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter says a Google Sheets spreadsheet can have up to 10 million cells. Google's Drive Help says up to 20 million cells or 100 MB for files in Google Sheets. The produce sheet of the chapter, 23,758 rows by 6 columns, is 142,548 cells, and clear() on it sends 617,746 bytes. Neither limit was approached [P01] [P26]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P26 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's 'one-time setup' does not hold for the setup it describes. Google's identity page says that an app whose publishing status is Testing and whose user type is External gets a refresh token that expires in seven days, and the help on the app audience adds a limit of 100 test users and the same seven days [P28] [P30]; when a refresh fails init() raises google.auth.exceptions.RefreshError (invalid_grant: Token has been expired or revoked), which the import swallows with a bare except and the first Spreadsheet() raises again, and the stale token-sheets.pickle is left in place, which was played against a local server and not against Google. The console has also changed: the consent-screen wizard of the chapter is now the pages Branding, Audience and Data Access of the Google Auth platform [P27], and Google's Python quickstart (Python 3.10.7 or later) stores a token.json file and not a pickle [P17]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P17, chx:P27, chx:P28, chx:P30 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The token files are Python pickles that the library loads with pickle.load when it is imported, and a pickle runs code when loaded. A token-sheets.pickle made to call exec wrote a marker file during import ezsheets, before any call. Python's documentation says the pickle module is not secure, and OWASP and MITRE name deserialization of untrusted data as a weakness (CWE-502). The chapter warns that shared credentials let others read the spreadsheets; it does not say that a token file received from someone else is a program. The pickle protocol of the files is the interpreter's default, 5 from Python 3.14 and 4 before [P01] [P39] [P52] [P53]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P39, chx:P52, chx:P53 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The sign-in is the loopback flow of RFC 8252 with a PKCE challenge (RFC 7636): the authorization URL the library builds has response_type=code, a code_challenge with method S256, access_type=offline and the redirect http://localhost:8080/, and with BROWSER=true the import printed the URL and listened on port 8080 (killed after the first line). With a credentials file and no browser the library raises webbrowser.Error (could not locate runnable browser), swallows it at import and raises it again at the first Spreadsheet(), and prints no URL, so a script on a server cannot sign in unless BROWSER is set. The scopes it asks for are the whole spreadsheets scope and the whole drive scope; Google rates drive as restricted, spreadsheets as sensitive and recommends drive.file where it is enough [P16] [P29] [P43] [P49] [P50]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P16, chx:P29, chx:P43, chx:P49, chx:P50 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Every cell write carries valueInputOption=USER_ENTERED, which Google documents as parsing the value as if a person had typed it into the cell, while RAW would store it as given; values.get returns FORMATTED_VALUE unless told otherwise. So the text written by a program may become a number, a date or a formula in Google, and what a read returns is the formatted text. The stand-in keeps text, so the effect itself was NOT EXECUTED; what was executed is the request the library sends and the request that RAW would send through the library's client [P09] [P11] [P12] [P13]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P09, chx:P11, chx:P12, chx:P13 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "A write is one request and a read after the first load is none, but a size change is three: sheet.rowCount += 1 sent a read of the properties, a read of the values and a batchUpdate, so Step 2 of Project 11 costs six requests for one transaction. Because the new row number is computed from a local rowCount, two programs that append at once write into the same row and the second overwrites the first with no error (a lost update, run deterministically with two Spreadsheet objects). The audit of Step 1 stops with ValueError on the first empty row of the grid, because getRows returns every row, and an unquoted name makes sys.argv[1:] four items and raises 'too many values to unpack (expected 3, got 4)'. Google's values guide describes an append call, which the library does not use [P01] [P07] [P09] [P18]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07, chx:P09, chx:P18 .
chx:F12 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Several behaviours of the library are surprises a course should name. getIdFromUrl cuts an id by position, so a URL that ends after the id loses its last character and the /spreadsheets/u/3/d/ form raises ValueError; two Sheet objects are equal when their sheetId is, which is 0 for the first sheet of every new spreadsheet; the in operator searches the text of the coordinates, so 'Alice' in sheet is False; Sheet.find does nothing; update(None) writes null because its line value == '' is a comparison; the download methods never close the file they open (ResourceWarning); listSpreadsheets does not exclude the Trash; and ranges are built unquoted, although Google's page says single quotes are required for a sheet name with spaces or special characters, and whether Google accepts the unquoted form was NOT EXECUTED [P08] [P22] [P03]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P03, chx:P08, chx:P22 .
chx:F13 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The library's state is thin. The readthedocs page is dated November 2019 and names ten of the 35 public names of the installed library (not getRow, downloadAs*, listSpreadsheets or delete); release 2026.4.28 differs from 2025.11.11 only in its version string; the classifiers stop at Python 3.13 and no Requires-Python is declared; the licence is GPLv3 or later; it sits on google-api-python-client 2.201.0, whose static discovery document for Sheets v4 had revision 20260921 where Google's live document had 20261005. gspread is the other library a course may name [P02] [P03] [P33] [P34] [P37] [P38]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P02, chx:P03, chx:P33, chx:P34, chx:P37, chx:P38 .
chx:F14 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "An advanced course on chapter 15 should add what the chapter leaves out: run the library against a stand-in so that every request can be inspected without an account; treat the token files as code; know that the Testing status makes the sign-in weekly and that the scopes are broad; read Google's quota page instead of the chapter's numbers and treat IGNORE_QUOTA as a throttle switch; batch writes (updateRows) instead of writing cells one at a time; guard the append against a second writer and the audit against an empty row; and pin the release, because the behaviour has changed between 2024.8.9 and 2025.11.11. What could not be run, any call with an OAuth token, is listed as NOT EXECUTED where it comes up [P01] [P07] [P28] [P39]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07, chx:P28, chx:P39 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "68 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "60 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-12T12:00:00; 34 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
